    "tarball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/freerouting/freerouting/tarball/v2.5.0",
    "zipball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/freerouting/freerouting/zipball/v2.5.0",
    "body": "> The continuation of Freerouting's development depends directly on the financial support of the project. If you feel that Freerouting has helped you, saved you time, or delivered value to your PCB designs, please consider supporting my work by [sponsoring me on GitHub](https://github.com/sponsors/andrasfuchs) or contributing via my [Stripe donation page](https://www.freerouting.app/donate). Every contribution directly enables me to keep dedicating time to tackling increasingly complex PCB layouts, delivering seamless, zero-install EDA integrations, and advancing routing tools for both human designers and automated AI workflows.\r\n\r\nFreerouting v2.5.0 is the biggest jump in routing performance and stability in Freerouting's history. Over the last month, I completely overhauled post-route optimization into a deterministic engine, introduced a canonical V2 continuous scoring model, integrated copper pour and power plane awareness, eliminated false-positive DRC artifacts, and introduced native CLI binaries alongside a modern KiCad 10+ IPC extension mode.\r\n\r\nCrucially, every improvement in this release is empirically measured and validated against PCBench, a dedicated benchmark and regression prevention harness testing 1,157 real-world open-source PCB designs.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Key Performance Highlights (Compared to v2.4.1)\r\n\r\nAcross the full 1,157-board corpus under identical per-board timeout budgets:\r\n\r\n* **Clean Boards Jumped from 30.9% to 75.0% (0 DRC Violations):** Clean board completion with zero clearance violations surged from **358 / 1,157 (30.9%)** in v2.4.1 to **868 / 1,157 (75.0%)** in v2.5.0. That is an increase of **+510 clean designs (+142% relative increase)**.\r\n* **Fully Routed Boards Rose from 51.9% to 75.4%:** Boards completed with 100% connectivity increased from **600 / 1,157 (51.9%)** in v2.4.1 to **872 / 1,157 (75.4%)** in v2.5.0 (+272 fully routed boards).\r\n* **Higher Overall Routing Quality:** Evaluated under Freerouting's canonical V2 continuous scoring engine, average score reached **969.2** (up from 943.9 in v2.4.1).\r\n* **Faster Total Corpus Execution:** Total corpus runtime dropped from **3,251.0 s** down to **2,997.1 s**, delivering an overall speedup while routing significantly more traces to completion.\r\n* **Breakthrough Progress on Routine Boards (Tier B):** On the 560 standard 2\u20134 layer production boards in Tier B, clean completion rose from 3.4% (19 / 560) to **61.6% (345 / 560)** \u2014 a leap of **+326 clean designs**, while fully routed boards more than doubled from 28.8% (161 / 560) to **62.1% (348 / 560)** and average score improved from 916.5 to **955.0**.\r\n* **Substantial Complex & Multi-Layer Gains (Tier C):** On dense 6+ layer boards, clean completion more than tripled from 14.8% (18 / 122) to **49.2% (60 / 122)**, fully routed boards increased from 27.0% (33 / 122) to **50.0% (61 / 122)**, and average score improved from 891.8 to **922.1**.\r\n* **High-Stress Diagnostic Resilience (Tier D):** On large stress boards, clean completion went from 0% (0 / 22) to **50.0% (11 / 22)** and fully routed designs rose from 18.2% (4 / 22) to **50.0% (11 / 22)**, with average score jumping from 905.4 to **961.6**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Robust Empirical Testing: The PCBench Framework\r\n\r\nPrior to this release, evaluating autorouter improvements relied on manual spot-checks or a small handful of synthetic fixtures, making it easy for localized changes to introduce subtle regressions elsewhere.\r\n\r\nTo prevent regressions and systematically guide development, I established **PCBench**, an automated benchmark harness testing **1,157 real-world PCB designs** categorized into four difficulty tiers:\r\n\r\n* **Tier A (453-Board Canary Gate):** Fast-solving production boards verified to solve with zero unrouted connections and zero DRC violations. Under v2.5.0, Tier A achieves a **99.8% clean completion rate (452 / 453)** and **99.8% fully routed rate (452 / 453)** with an average score of **999.8 / 1000** (up from 70.9% clean and 88.7% fully routed in v2.4.1).\r\n* **Tier B (560 Routine Production Boards):** Standard 2\u20134 layer designs that present active routing, escape, and optimization challenges, giving clear empirical visibility into routing yield.\r\n* **Tier C (122 Complex & Multi-Layer Boards):** High-density 6+ layer boards testing multi-layer fanout, plane traversal, and via optimization.\r\n* **Tier D (22 High-Stress Boards):** Massive net-count and large surface-area boards testing spatial data structures and memory boundaries.\r\n\r\nFull per-board logs, metrics, and comparisons are browsable in the [PCBench interactive web report](https://freerouting.org/benchmarks) and in-repo at `scripts/benchmark/results/benchmarks.md`.\r\n\r\n### Corpus Benchmark Summary (All 1,157 Fixtures)\r\n\r\n| Version | Fixtures | Clean (0 DRC) | Fully Routed | Timeouts | Failures | Total Time (s) | Avg. Score (V2) |\r\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\r\n| **v2.4.1** | 1,157 | 358 / 1,157 (30.9%) | 600 / 1,157 (51.9%) | 78 / 1,157 (6.7%) | 0 / 1,157 (0.0%) | 3,251.0 | 943.9 |\r\n| **v2.5.0** | **1,157** | **868 / 1,157 (75.0%)** | **872 / 1,157 (75.4%)** | **78 / 1,157 (6.7%)** | **0 / 1,157 (0.0%)** | **2,997.1** | **969.2** |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Native CLI Executables (GraalVM PGO)\r\n\r\nStarting with v2.5.0, Freerouting provides standalone, native command-line executables compiled via GraalVM Native Image with Profile-Guided Optimization (PGO) for:\r\n* Linux x64\r\n* macOS Apple Silicon (arm64)\r\n* macOS Intel (x64)\r\n* Windows x64\r\n\r\nThese native binaries eliminate the Java JRE requirement for CLI users, CI/CD runners, and EDA integrations, cutting startup overhead from several seconds down to sub-second instantaneous execution.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Modern KiCad 10+ IPC Extension Mode\r\n\r\nFreerouting v2.5.0 introduces direct Protocol Buffers IPC integration for KiCad 10+:\r\n* **Modern Background Progress Dialog:** A native-feeling progress window with live stage updates (Fanout, Autoroute, Optimize), real-time score display, and responsive cancellation.\r\n* **Non-Blocking Background Routing:** Routing runs in a separate process without freezing KiCad's user interface.\r\n* **Robust Hardware Support:** Full support for non-copper mounting holes (NPTH) and planeless power nets.\r\n* **Reliable Fallback:** Seamless fallback to the battle-tested Specctra DSN/SES pipeline if IPC is unavailable.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Unified Deterministic Route Optimizer (`BatchOptimizer`)\r\n\r\nThe post-routing optimizer has been consolidated into a single canonical `BatchOptimizer` engine shared across Desktop GUI, CLI, and REST API pipelines:\r\n* **Deterministic Execution:** Results are consistent and repeatable across runs. A strict total order comparator (`ItemRouteResult.compareTo`) with deterministic item-ID tie-breaking guarantees candidate selection is stable.\r\n* **Guaranteed Best-Board Preservation:** The optimizer benchmarks baseline board state before pass 1. If subsequent passes fail to find an improvement or degrade quality, the best-known board is automatically restored before completion.\r\n* **Reduced Memory Footprint:** Bounded candidate evaluation and lazy board copying significantly lower memory pressure and GC churn on large, dense designs.\r\n* **Pre-Flight Guards & Fast Canary Exit:** The router skips fruitless optimization passes on incomplete boards, and boards that cannot improve exit within seconds via canary failure limits.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Algorithmic, DRC & Geometric Advancements\r\n\r\n* **Copper Pour & Power Plane Awareness (#152):** Full awareness of existing polygon copper pours and power planes during routing and clearance checks.\r\n* **Planeless Power Layer Routing (#935):** Power nets on inner layers without predefined polygon pours now route traces cleanly instead of leaving pins unrouted.\r\n* **Pre-Existing DRC Violation Categorization (#930):** Violations already present in the unrouted input PCB (such as pad overlaps or tight connector footprints) are detected, categorized, and separated from router-introduced violations, preventing the router from misjudging board legality.\r\n* **Balanced Merge for Plane Cutouts (#928):** Thanks to a contribution by Patrick Reybaud, plane cutout subtraction in `getAwtArea` now uses balanced tree merging, providing an 8x to 51x rendering speedup on copper planes with hundreds of cutout holes.\r\n* **Corner Clearance Validation:** Full tile-shape corner checks for edge connectors and castellated pads. Default copper-to-edge clearance is capped cleanly at 250 \u00b5m.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Dual Scoring Model (V2 Continuous)\r\n\r\nFreerouting separates routing completion from post-route copper efficiency into two independent 0\u20131000 scores:\r\n* **Router Score (`V2_CONTINUOUS`):** Measures board completion and design rule legality. Finishing the second half of connections is weighted higher than the first half, penalizing clearance violations and shortfalls accurately.\r\n* **Optimizer Score (`V2_LOWER_BOUND`):** Evaluates copper efficiency against placement-derived Manhattan minimum spanning tree lower bounds ($L_{\\min}, V_{\\min}, B_{\\min}$).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## API, MCP & Agent Telemetry\r\n\r\n* **1-Turn Composite Autorouting (`POST /v1/autoroute` & MCP `autoroute_board`):** AI coding agents and external scripts can now provision sessions, upload multi-file inputs, route, and receive multi-format exports in a single call.\r\n* **Dual-User Attribution:** Telemetry now differentiates between GUI, CLI, REST API, and MCP pipelines, as well as Human and AI Agent actors.\r\n* **Context-Optimized DRC Summaries:** Natural-language violation explanations with hotspot clustering and layout repair hints; `compact=true` drops raw coordinate bloat by >70% for LLMs.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Breaking Changes & Deprecations\r\n\r\n* **Removed Optimizer Strategies:** `-hr` (`hybridRatio`), and strategy values `RANDOM`, `GREEDY`, and `HYBRID` for `-us`/`-is` have been removed. Use `-us GLOBAL_OPTIMAL` with `-is SEQUENTIAL` or `-is PRIORITIZED`.\r\n* **API Normalized Score Scale:** API `normalized_score` now outputs the 0\u20131000 canonical router score rather than a 0\u20131 fraction.\r\n* **Flat Setting Keys:** Nested keys under `--router.autorouter.*` are preferred. Legacy flat keys (e.g. `--router.max_passes`) remain supported with deprecation warnings.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## What's Next\r\n\r\nWith v2.5.0 establishing an empirically verified baseline, my immediate focus turns to:\r\n1. **Continuous Routing Improvements on Large & Complex Boards:** I am committed to continuously delivering even better results with bigger, more complicated boards, driving further gains in completion rates and routing quality on dense multi-layer layouts.\r\n2. **KiCad IPC Real-Time Feedback & Control:** Throttled live trace canvas streaming so users see traces route directly inside KiCad in real-time, along with enhanced interactive control featuring start, pause, stop, and one-click undo operations.\r\n3. **Retire KiCad SWIG in Favor of Zero-Install Native IPC:** In the next release, I will completely remove legacy KiCad SWIG support and transition fully to the modern KiCad IPC architecture powered by native CLI executables, completely removing the requirement for users to install Java 25.\r\n4. **Escape & Congestion Fanout:** New escape routing algorithms tailored for high-density BGA packages and power nets.\r\n5. **Multi-Threaded Autorouting:** Exploring concurrency in the primary maze-search expansion phase while preserving determinism, under the strict condition that it will only be integrated if it is proven to make the router objectively better and faster.\r\n\r\nThank you to everyone in the EDA, KiCad, and open-source hardware communities who contributed bug reports, board designs, and pull requests to help make this release possible!\r\n",
